7. PROCESS PLATFORMER: GAMEPLAY (BUILD EVERY DETAIL)
=====================================================================
Use Phaser 3 with Arcade Physics (same loading approach as Concept Runner). If
Phaser cannot work reliably, use HTML canvas. Put the game in its own
component (ProcessPlatformerGame) using the shared interface from section 3.
The component must destroy its Phaser instance when unmounted and must never
create two instances at once.

PAGE LAYOUT
- HUD (DOM, above the canvas): 3 pixel hearts, score, streak ("Streak x3"),
  round label ("Round 1 of 2"), progress ("Step 3 of 6"), Hint button showing
  remaining hints ("Hint (2)"), Info, Pause, and Mute buttons.
- Instruction panel (DOM): the round's title and instruction in readable body
  text.
- "Sequence so far" panel (DOM): N numbered slots in order. Empty slots show
  the number and "?". A correctly placed step fills its slot with its label
  (green outline). This tells the student how many steps there are and shows
  the process building up.
- Canvas below (or beside on wide screens), 16:9, scales to the container
  width (Phaser Scale FIT, minimum 320 px wide), pixel-art rendering
  (image-rendering: pixelated, antialias off).
- Touch controls on touch devices: large Left, Right, and Jump buttons plus a
  Hint button. Prevent page scrolling while touching the canvas or buttons.

WORLD (virtual resolution 960 x 540, scaled to the container)
- One screen, no scrolling, dungeon background with at least 2 parallax layers
  (disabled in reduce-motion mode), a solid stone GROUND along the bottom at
  y = 510 that the player can always walk on. NO pits, NO instant-death zones,
  so the player can never get stuck.
- A START sign at the far left of the ground where the player spawns.
- All art is code-drawn (rectangles and simple pixel sprites); no image files.
  Platforms look like stone or wooden slabs with a visible top surface and the
  step text printed on them.

PLATFORM LAYOUT ALGORITHM (always reachable, never overlapping, readable)
- P = number of steps (+1 for a challenge round with a decoy). P is 2 to 9.
- Platform size 180 x 48 virtual pixels (minimum 140 wide if P is more than 7).
- Three tiers at y = 390, 280, and 170. Spread platforms over the tiers as
  evenly as possible, filling the lower tiers first when it does not divide
  evenly (7 platforms = 3 low, 2 middle, 2 high).
- Within a tier, spread platforms evenly over x from 40 to 920 with a gap of
  40 to 160 between neighbors. Stagger tiers by 40 px alternately so they do
  not stack in columns. Add random jitter of up to 12 px in x and y without
  breaking the gap rules.
- Reachability (verify in code, re-roll the layout if violated): the vertical
  difference between adjacent tiers is at most 120 (jump height is about 150),
  and every platform has a neighbor within 200 px horizontally.
- Assignment: shuffle which step sits on which platform. Re-shuffle until the
  steps are NOT in correct order when read left to right and top to bottom, and
  step 1 is NOT on the lowest tier closest to the start sign. The decoy is
  placed like any other platform.
- Text on platforms wraps to up to 3 lines and auto-shrinks to a minimum of 11
  CSS px equivalent. If it still does not fit, truncate with an ellipsis ON THE
  CANVAS ONLY, because the full text of every step is always available in the
  DOM.
- DOM list of all platform labels in shuffled order (readable by screen
  readers, and visible through a small "Show steps list" toggle under the
  canvas) so nothing is ever cut off.
- If layout generation fails 10 times in a row, fall back to a simple evenly
  spaced two-tier layout.

PLAYER AND PHYSICS (gentle and forgiving)
- Pixel character with idle, run, and jump animation (2 to 4 frames or a
  bobbing effect).
- Gravity 900, run speed 220, jump height about 150 virtual pixels, variable
  jump height (releasing the key early cuts the jump), coyote time 100 ms,
  jump buffering 100 ms.
- Platforms are ONE-WAY: the player can jump up through them and only collides
  when falling onto the top.
- Invisible walls keep the player on screen.

HOW A STEP IS CHOSEN (walking across platforms must not count as answering)
- A platform is chosen only when the player STANDS on it for 600 ms without
  leaving it. A small filling bar on the platform edge shows the progress.
  Leaving the platform cancels and resets the bar.
- The intro card says: "Stand on a platform for a moment to choose it."
- Platforms already placed correctly cannot be chosen again; they stay as safe
  platforms to walk on.
- Every choice is checked against the next expected step.

CORRECT CHOICE
- The platform turns green and keeps a badge with its sequence number, "+100"
  floats up, happy sound, the matching slot in "Sequence so far" fills, the
  streak increases by one.
- A NON-BLOCKING caption under the canvas shows the step's explanation for 3
  seconds (the game does not pause). It is also announced through aria-live.

WRONG CHOICE (a real step in the wrong place, or the decoy)
- The platform shakes for 400 ms, then crumbles and falls; the player drops to
  the ground; one life is lost (not in practice mode); the streak resets to 0;
  red flash and lose-life sound (no screen shake in reduce-motion mode). Call
  onLivesChange.
- A BLOCKING explanation panel stops the game until the student presses
  "Continue" or Enter, showing:
  * for a wrong real step: "That step comes later." and "Next step: [label of
    the expected step]" with its explanation. Do NOT reveal which platform it is
    on.
  * for the decoy: "That doesn't belong in this process." and the decoy's
    whyNot.
  * "From the PDF: [pageLabel] N" (use the course's pageLabel if the app has
    one, otherwise "Page") with the source quote in italics.
- After Continue, the crumbled platform RESPAWNS after 1.5 seconds in the same
  place (fade-in with a dust effect). If the player is standing there, wait
  until the spot is free. The round always stays solvable.
- The student may keep trying the same step until correct or out of lives. Only
  the FIRST choice for each step counts for scoring and mastery.

HINTS
- 2 hints per round (Hint button or key H). A hint makes the platform with the
  correct next step pulse with a bright outline for 3 seconds.
- Outside practice mode a hint costs 25 points (score never below 0), and that
  step counts as NOT correct first try (answer marked "hinted"). A correct
  choice after a hint gives +25 instead of +100 and no streak increase.
- In practice mode hints are free and unlimited in effect (no point cost).

ROUND COMPLETION
- When the last real step is placed, play a short celebration and show a
  blocking "Sequence complete" panel with the full ordered list of steps and
  explanations, the page tag with the source quote, and a "Continue" button.
- Bonus +150 points if the round had no wrong choices and no hints.
- Then the next round starts with a new layout. After the last round the
  segment ends.

CHALLENGE ROUND EXTRAS
- A banner at the start: "Challenge: one platform does NOT belong. Don't step
  on it."
- The decoy looks identical to real steps. Choosing it is a wrong choice (the
  explanation uses whyNot).
- Placing all real steps ends the round. The decoy platform then turns red and
  the "Sequence complete" panel also lists it as "Not part of the process" with
  its whyNot text.

CONTROLS (all must work)
- Keyboard: Left/Right or A/D move; Up, W, or Space jump; H hint; P or Escape
  pause.
- Touch: on-screen Left, Right, Jump (hold to move), and Hint buttons.
- Pause overlay with "Resume" and "Quit level". The game auto-pauses when the
  tab loses focus.

SOUND (Web Audio API, no audio files; use the existing sound helper and the
existing mute setting "studyquest_muted"; start only after the first user
interaction): jump, land (soft), correct, wrong, crumble, round complete. Add
them to the existing helper instead of creating a second one.

ACCESSIBILITY AND SIMPLE MODE
- Respect the existing Reduce motion setting and prefers-reduced-motion:
  disable parallax and screen shake and slow the player slightly.
- SIMPLE MODE (an alternative to jumping), toggled from the Info card and the
  HUD, remembered under "studyquest_simple_platformer". The round shows the
  steps as large shuffled cards. The student taps or presses them (keys 1 to 9)
  in the order they believe is correct. It uses EXACTLY the same rules:
  first-try scoring, wrong choices and lives, explanations, hints, decoy
  handling, rounds, "Sequence so far" panel, and results. No standing delay is
  needed.
- If Phaser cannot start, switch automatically to simple mode with the message
  "The game could not load. Switching to simple mode."
- aria-live announcements for every correct and wrong choice, round start, and
  round completion.
- Every control is reachable by keyboard with a visible focus outline.

SCORING SUMMARY (consistent with the other games)
- +100 per step correct first time, with the streak bonus (+25 per streak
  step, capped at +100 on that step), +25 for a step that was hinted or
  corrected, +150 round bonus, -25 per hint outside practice mode (floor 0).
- XP follows the existing rule: 10 XP per first-try-correct step (none for
  hinted steps), plus the existing level completion bonuses.

